    "result_text": "The full build is green. The only executable test class in the project is `AcceptanceTest` (the others are a base class and a test utility), and it passes along with the whole build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` field so that a newly created owner reports `true` when its city already holds 40\u201349 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50), leaving the existing hard rejection at 50 unchanged.\n\nChanges:\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `capacityWarning` Boolean field (mapped to a `capacity_warning` column) with getter/setter.\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it implicitly (same as the existing `bulkSignupWarning`).\n3. **Schema files** (`h2`, `hsqldb`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column.\n4. **New function `FlagCapacityWarning.java`** \u2014 mirrors `EnsureCityCapacity`'s case-insensitive/trimmed city counting; it runs before `SaveOwner` (so the new owner isn't counted yet) and sets `capacityWarning = count >= 40 && count < 50`.\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `flagCapacityWarning` step into the create pipeline (after `flagBulkSignup`, before `flagPossibleDuplicate`), under the same `transaction` governance.\n\nThe hard 409 rejection stays in `EnsureCityCapacity` (`count >= 50`), which runs earlier in the pipeline, so the flag step never observes a count of 50+.",
